This one avoids recursion. A stack keeps the nodes in postorder, and a `Map` stores the height already found for each node. Once both children have been processed, the current node can be checked.

The extra map makes the iterative version a little longer, but it also avoids a deep recursive call stack. The original tree is only read.

```javascript
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *      constructor(val = 0, left = null, right = null){
 *          this.data = val;
 *          this.left = null;
 *          this.right = null;
 *      }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    isBalanced(root) {
        if (root === null) {
            return true;
        }

        const stack = [[root, false]];
        const heights = new Map();

        while (stack.length > 0) {
            const [node, visited] = stack.pop();

            if (node === null) {
                continue;
            }

            if (!visited) {
                stack.push([node, true]);
                stack.push([node.right, false]);
                stack.push([node.left, false]);
                continue;
            }

            const leftHeight = node.left === null ? 0 : heights.get(node.left);
            const rightHeight = node.right === null ? 0 : heights.get(node.right);

            if (Math.abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) {
                return false;
            }

            heights.set(node, 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight));
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is pushed and processed a constant number of times.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and height map.

Kept the direct version here. For each node, I calculate the height of both children, compare them, and then check the two child subtrees. It follows the definition closely, but the same lower subtree can be measured many times.

### Dry run
For a chain of three nodes, the bottom node has height `1`. Its parent gets left and right heights `1` and `0`, so it is still fine. At the root, the heights become `2` and `0`, and the tree is rejected.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution{
    int height(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + std::max(height(node->left), height(node->right));
    }

    bool checkEverySubtree(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        int leftHeight = height(node->left);
        int rightHeight = height(node->right);

        if (std::abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) {
            return false;
        }

        return checkEverySubtree(node->left) &&
               checkEverySubtree(node->right);
    }

public:
    bool isBalanced(TreeNode *root){
        return checkEverySubtree(root);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because height is recalculated at many nodes.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack, where `h` is the tree height. The input tree is not changed.

Here the recursive function always returns the height, and a boolean field remembers if any node fails the balance test. This is easy to follow because height calculation stays separate from the final answer.

### Edge cases
A null root returns true. A height gap of exactly one is allowed, while a gap of two makes the result false. The code does not modify any node or child link.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

public class Solution
{
    private bool balanced;

    public bool IsBalanced(TreeNode root)
    {
        balanced = true;
        GetHeight(root);
        return balanced;
    }

    private int GetHeight(TreeNode node)
    {
        if (node == null)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        int leftHeight = GetHeight(node.left);
        int rightHeight = GetHeight(node.right);

        if (Math.Abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1)
        {
            balanced = false;
        }

        return 1 + Math.Max(leftHeight, rightHeight);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one visit per node.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the DFS call stack, worst case `O(n)` for a one-sided tree.

The helper returns more than just true or false. A non-negative result is the subtree height, while `-1` means an imbalance was found. This lets the parent stop immediately instead of doing more work on a bad subtree.

An empty tree returns height `0`, so it is treated as balanced. A single node also passes because both child heights are zero.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        return getHeight(root) != -1;
    }

    private int getHeight(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int leftHeight = getHeight(node.left);
        if (leftHeight == -1) {
            return -1;
        }

        int rightHeight = getHeight(node.right);
        if (rightHeight == -1) {
            return -1;
        }

        if (Math.abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each node is visited once.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive calls. In a skewed tree, `h` can be `n`.

